# ...but that bare pattern matches EVERY moon.jpg in the tree, and it
# silently swallowed the Moon's baked hero tile — the file was on disk,
# the bake reported it, and it simply never reached a commit.
!assets/hero/moon.jpg
/music/
*.orig

# USGS raw shapefiles (297 MB, exceeds GitHub's 100MB-per-file limit).
# Only the generated PNG + JSON in the parent assets/ folder are needed
# at runtime. Re-download from USGS Astrogeology if you need to rebuild.
planet_explorer/moon/viewer/assets/geology/Unified_Geologic_Map_of_the_Moon_GIS_v2/

# Local restore points created during the flight-sim merge (not for the repo)
.merge-backup-*/
assets/etna_viewer_demo_audio.webm

# GALES solver source tree (placed locally; huge, has its own .git)
GeoID_GIS/gales/

# Big local media and caches that live beside the site but are not part of it.
# A bare "git add -A" once swept these into a commit -- a 927 MB video among
# them -- and GitHub's pre-receive hook declined the push, silently, for days.
*.avi
*.mp4
*.webm
*.npy

# Bake caches: the source data a service script downloads, never served.
GeoID_GIS/services/.cache/

# The glacier bake's downloads and merge — 400 MB of RGI tarballs.
.glacier-bake/

# Bake WORKING DIRECTORIES — downloads, extracted sources and tile bands.
# The pyramid under data/global/<name>/ is the artefact; these are the scratch
# that produced it, and 376 MB of the soil bake reached a commit before this
# rule existed. Each bake script recreates its own from the source.
data/global/.*-work/

# GLiM's pyramid — 169 MB, HELD OUT pending the object-storage decision.
#
# Not ignored because it is scratch (it is the artefact), but because the repo
# is already past the 1 GB GitHub Pages limit and this would add another 169 MB
# to a history that cannot be shrunk without a rewrite. It was swept into a
# commit once by `git add -A` after the bake finished, which is precisely why
# an explicit rule is safer than remembering.
#
# The layer reports this honestly: with the pyramid absent the row refuses to
# tick and says its tiles could not be read. Delete this rule to ship it in the
# repo, or point `tiles_base` at the bucket once one exists.
# The TILES only. `manifest.json` and `classes.json` stay with the site: the
# manifest is 10-20 KB and carries `has()` and every tile's size, which is what
# chooses a zoom before anything is fetched — so it has to be readable even when
# the bucket is not.
data/global/glim/[0-9]*/

# The soil pyramid's TILES, for the same reason and on the same terms as GLiM's
# above: 562 tiles / 27 MB now served from the bucket
# (`publish-tiles.py soil --base https://data.geoidinitiative.com/soil`), with
# `manifest.json` and `units.json` staying with the site so `has()`, every
# tile's size and the legend are readable before — and without — a fetch.
data/global/soil/[0-9]*/

# The geology and glacier pyramids' TILES, on the same terms as GLiM and soil
# above: 1,089 + 806 tiles / ~128 MB now served from the bucket. Their
# `manifest.json` (and ice's `names.json` / `thickness.json` legend sidecars)
# stay with the site, because `has()`, every tile's size and the naming table
# are read before — and without — a tile fetch.
data/global/geology/[0-9]*/
data/global/ice/[0-9]*/
